From dc211b3e8509202a87539679f0f8e499f8a88a9e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aubrey Pule Khoza <aubreypk@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 11:25:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Make the interface read as a game rather than an admin
 console

Dark palette, larger type, and the activity list grouped by learning outcome
instead of forty-two rows in one column. Seven collapsible cards, each showing
how many are solved and the level spread for the learner in play; the first
group with unfinished work opens on load.

The design console and the model page take the same palette and type scale but
keep plain surfaces and dense tables. They are where a lecturer reads the
model's verdicts, and legibility matters more than atmosphere there.

Colour was checked rather than chosen by eye. All 68 foreground and background
pairs meet WCAG 2.1 AA against the surface each actually sits on: body text
clears 13:1, the dimmest secondary text 4.68:1, every level chip 6.4:1, and
control borders clear 3:1 for non-text contrast. Layer 2 of the evaluation
audits this interface automatically, so a palette that merely looked right
would be the thing under test rather than the design. No webfont, for the same
reason -- a display face would suit the subject and cost a render-blocking
request in the Lighthouse run Chapter 6 reports.

The grouping needed the outcome on each row, so /api/activities now returns it.
No activity in either curriculum spans two outcomes.

Collapsing uses details and summary rather than component state, so keyboard
and screen-reader behaviour comes from the browser instead of being
reimplemented.

Checked: 33 API checks pass on both curricula; every class the JSX references
is defined in the stylesheet; contrast verified against every surface.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01FSDM1Wo7WiT7h5vJNaY23D
